course: dictionary
lesson: 6194
title: Misinformation or disinformation?
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Misinformation is wrong or false information, often shared by mistake. «Misinformation is false information shared by mistake» means the person sharing it did not know it was false. The "mis-" at the start is a clue: it means wrong, as in misread or misspell.
explain: Disinformation is different. It is false information spread on purpose to trick people. People often mix the two words up, so check which one fits. «Disinformation is made on purpose to trick.»
explain: The key question is: did the person sharing it believe it was true? «Misinformation is false information shared by someone who thinks it is true.» A mistake that is fixed openly with a correction is not a lie: «a mistake fixed openly with a correction is not a lie.»
fact: Misinformation means wrong or false information, often spread by mistake.
fact: Disinformation means false information spread on purpose to trick people.
fact: Misinformation spreads fast online, especially about health, wars and elections.
fact: To tackle misinformation, look at the source, ask «who made this and why?», and compare it with trusted news.
fact: A fact-check is a careful look at whether a claim is true before you share it.
exercise: «Misinformation is false information shared by mistake; disinformation is false information spread on purpose.» What does misinformation mean? | Information that has been checked and is always true | * Wrong or false information, often shared by mistake | Information spread on purpose to trick people | A kind of radio programme
exercise: Which word means false information spread on purpose to trick people? | Misinformation | Correction | Source | * Disinformation
exercise: A news site posts a fact, then openly corrects its mistake the next day. Is the correction a lie? | Yes, because the first post was true | Yes, because any change to a story is a lie | Yes, it is disinformation spread on purpose | * No, a mistake fixed openly with a correction is not a lie
test: Before you share a health story, what should you do first? | Spread it to tackle misinformation | Share it quickly so your friends see it first | Trust it because it is online | * Look at the source and ask who made it and why
test: Which phrase means trying to stop false information from spreading? | * Tackle misinformation | Spread misinformation | Trust every post | Ignore the source
exam: «Misinformation is false information shared by someone who thinks it is true; disinformation is made on purpose to trick.» A false post was shared by someone who really believed it. Which word fits best? | * Misinformation | Fact-check | Correction | Disinformation
